mailbox.resource/MB_GetEDIDBlock               mailbox.resource/MB_GetEDIDBlock

   NAME
	MB_GetEDIDBlock -- read one 128-byte EDID block (V1.1)

   SYNOPSIS
	status = MB_GetEDIDBlock( block_number, edid_out )
	d0                        d0            a0

	ULONG MB_GetEDIDBlock(ULONG, UBYTE *);

   FUNCTION
	Reads one 128-byte EDID block from the currently connected
	display, using property tag GET_EDID_BLOCK (0x00030020). Block 0
	is the base EDID block; blocks 1 and up are CEA extension
	blocks, whose count is announced in byte 126 of block 0.

   INPUTS
	block_number    The EDID block to read (0 = base block).
	edid_out        A pointer to a 128-byte buffer to receive the block,
	                or NULL to discard it.

   RESULTS
	status          0 on success, or -1 on a mailbox transport error or a
	                firmware-reported failure.

   EXAMPLES
	Read the base EDID block plus any CEA extension blocks it
	announces (adapted from Emu68DisplayDevice's Main.c, which built this
	same request by hand on top of MB_RawCommand() before this dedicated
	function existed):

	    #include <exec/types.h>
	    #include <proto/exec.h>
	    #include <proto/mailbox.h>
	    #include <resources/mailbox.h>

	    #define EDID_BLOCK_SIZE  (128)
	    #define EDID_BLOCK_COUNT (256)

	    extern APTR MailboxBase;
	    UBYTE edidData[EDID_BLOCK_SIZE * EDID_BLOCK_COUNT];

	    ULONG ReadEDID(VOID)
	    {
	        UBYTE *buffer = edidData;

	        if (MB_GetEDIDBlock(0, buffer) == 0)
	        {
	            UBYTE extensions = buffer[126];
	            buffer += EDID_BLOCK_SIZE;

	            if (extensions > 0 && extensions < EDID_BLOCK_COUNT)
	            {
	                ULONG block;

	                for (block = 0; block < extensions; block++)
	                {
	                    if (MB_GetEDIDBlock(block + 1, buffer) != 0)
	                        break;

	                    buffer += EDID_BLOCK_SIZE;
	                }
	            }
	        }

	        return (ULONG)(buffer - edidData);
	    }

	Unlike the hand-built MB_RawCommand() buffer this replaces, the EDID
	payload's own byte order never gets touched by MB_GetEDIDBlock() -- see
	the NOTE above.

   NOTE
	The EDID payload itself is a raw byte blob and is copied
	verbatim, without any byte-swapping -- only the tag's own status
	word is a real 32-bit value and is swapped internally.

   SEE ALSO

